  "title": "Opposite --prefer answers diverge silently",
  "parent": null,
  "seq": 113,
  "body": "Two copies of an unkeyed store each resolve the same field conflict with `--prefer ours` in the canonical merge. Each then reports \"already merged\" for the other's result, and `show` differs between them for good: nothing tells either copy they disagree. Older than 0.7.0 (the canonical merge's `--prefer` has always worked this way); not fixed by the 0.7.0 change that took status out of it.\n\nReproduction (dev build at the canonical status fix, scratch stores, 2026-11-19):\n\n1. `init` copy A, `add \"One\"`; copy `.tasks/` to B.\n2. A: `edit t1 -b alice`; B: `edit t1 -b bob`. Bring each copy's objects to the other.\n3. A: `merge <B's HEAD> --prefer ours` (merged, 2 replayed); B: `merge <A's HEAD> --prefer ours` (merged, 1 replayed).\n4. Bring objects both ways. A: `merge <B's new HEAD>` says \"already merged: everything on ... is on this line\"; B: `merge <A's new HEAD>` says the same.\n5. `show t1`: A's body is \"alice\", B's is \"bob\".\n\nWhy: the merge treats the other HEAD as held once every original it names is on our line (`replayed_from`), so a resolution recorded only as which side's patch applied never reaches the other copy. The status rule's version of this (opposite `--prefer` on a status) was removed from the canonical merge by the same fix; field conflicts remain.",
  "types": []
}
